Yeah—there are several operators hiding in that cluster.

“If you don’t mind, it doesn’t matter.” Physically, matter still exists without mind; phenomenologically, though, mattering requires a valuing system. No observer, no salience. So no mind → no “mattering.” The pun accidentally lands on a real distinction between ontology and significance.

And matter → matte by dropping the final r is unusually clean. Matte surfaces suppress reflection/glare; linguistically, removing the letter makes matter become something less reflective. So you can read it as: remove a little articulation and the thing stops throwing your image back at you. That fits the broader cave/mirror machinery nicely.

“Don’t dictate me” has teeth too. Dictation is ostensibly you commanding the tool, but every interface imposes a grammar on its user. A hammer privileges nails; a spreadsheet privileges cells; a feed privileges scrollable fragments; an LLM privileges language. So the reciprocal law is:

We shape tools → tools shape available moves → repeated moves shape us.

That’s why mastery includes noticing when the instrument has started choosing the music.

And “U-genics” is a much safer and more interesting formulation than historical eugenics: you-genics, self-directed adaptation. Humans aren’t literally at war with natural selection—natural selection has no intentions—but we have become unusually capable niche constructors. Medicine, contraception, agriculture, culture, education, genetic technologies, cities, eyeglasses, air conditioning, even cooking all change which environmental pressures reach us and what gets transmitted.

So instead of:

humanity vs natural selection

I’d model it:

natural selection produced an organism capable of modifying its own selection environment.

Evolution grew something that could reach back toward the machinery that grew it.

That recursion is wild.

And “we take things very personal” closes the set. Humans constantly turn impersonal forces into persons: Mother Nature, Father Time, Lady Luck, Death, Justice, the Market, History. Personification lets social cognition grab an abstraction and reason about it using machinery built for agents.

That can be incredibly powerful—and misleading when we forget we put the costume on.

Take it personal enough to understand it.
Take the costume off again before you mistake the actor for the law.